# .NET Framework → .NET 8 Migration: `EventProcessor`

## Critical Issues in the Original Code

| Issue | .NET Framework | .NET 8 |
|-------|---------------|--------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `ThreadAbortException` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Throws `ThreadInterruptedException` | Still works but **unreliable** — only interrupts managed blocking calls |
| `ManualResetEvent` + manual `lock` | Works but race-prone | Better primitives available |
| Lost signals | If `Set()` fires between `WaitOne()` and `Reset()`, items are missed | Same bug, but easier to fix with modern APIs |

## Race Condition in Original Code

```
Thread A (RunLoop)          Thread B (Enqueue)
─────────────────           ──────────────────
WaitOne() returns
                            Enqueue(item2)
                            Set()           ← signal lost!
Reset()                     
Dequeue() → item1
WaitOne() → blocks forever  ← item2 stuck
```

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _runTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _runTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
        {
            Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Channel is closed.");
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();          // signal no more items
        _cts?.Cancel();                      // cancel if blocked waiting
        if (_runTask is not null)
        {
            try { await _runTask; }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) { }
        }
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* ... */ }
}
```

## Key Migration Decisions

**1. `Thread` → `Task.Run`** — No need to manage raw threads. The `Task` integrates with `async/await` and the thread pool.

**2. `Thread.Abort/Interrupt` → `CancellationToken`** — Cooperative cancellation is the only supported pattern in modern .NET:

```csharp
// ❌ .NET Framework — abrupt termination
_thread.Abort();

// ✅ .NET 8 — cooperative cancellation
_cts.Cancel();          // signals the token
await _runTask;         // waits for clean exit
```

**3. `ManualResetEvent` + `lock` + `Queue` → `Channel<T>`** — One type replaces three, and eliminates the race condition:

```csharp
// ❌ Three primitives, manual synchronization, race-prone
_newEvent.WaitOne();
_newEvent.Reset();
lock (_lock) { item = _queue.Dequeue(); }

// ✅ Single thread-safe, async-ready primitive
await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
```

**4. `IAsyncDisposable`** — Ensures clean shutdown via `await using`:

```csharp
await using var processor = new EventProcessor();
processor.Start();
processor.Enqueue("work");
// DisposeAsync called automatically — drains queue, cancels, awaits
```

## If You Need a Synchronous API

Replace `Channel<T>` with `BlockingCollection<T>` (the synchronous equivalent):

```csharp
private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();

private void RunLoop()
{
    foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
        Handle(item);
}

public void Stop()
{
    _queue.CompleteAdding();
    _cts.Cancel();
    _thread.Join(5000); // no Abort needed — token exits the loop
}
```

## Pitfalls to Watch

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Swallowing cancellation** | Always catch `OperationCanceledException` at the runner boundary — don't let it crash the app |
| **Forgetting `Writer.Complete()`** | Without it, `ReadAllAsync` blocks forever — your app hangs on shutdown |
| **Fire-and-forget `Task.Run`** | Store the `Task` and `await` it on shutdown, otherwise exceptions vanish silently |
| **Unbounded channels** | `CreateUnbounded` has no backpressure — use `CreateBounded` if producers can outrun consumers |
